# Universe content · where the universe's other bodies are, and how many (made round you, all clickable)
# where: galaxy-heart (one at the middle of every galaxy), galaxy (among a galaxy's stars), between-galaxies (in the space
#   between them), belt (a star's asteroid belt), kuiper (its Kuiper belt), oort (its Oort cloud), inner (falling through
#   its inner system, near its worlds).
# per-cubic-ly: how many in a cubic light-year (among a galaxy's stars near the Sun there are about 0.004 stars a cubic
#   light-year; of every thousand stars about one ended as a black hole and one as a neutron star; there may be several free-
#   floating, rogue, planets for every star; between galaxies, intracluster stars are about a ten-thousandth as common as in
#   a galaxy); in a belt or cloud: shown, how many are made round you at a time.
# entry: the content entry it is (its card); size-km: how big it is drawn (a black hole: its glowing disc); colour.
# coma-km and tail-km: a comet's coma and tail at their real sizes (comas 100,000 km to over a million, tails tens of
#   millions of km), each comet its own in the range: a comet shows by its tail across its whole system.
# shape: what it is drawn as close in: rock (a lumpy body lit by its star, cratered), comet (a dark nucleus in its coma of
#   gas and dust, its tail pushed straight away from the star); without one, a point of light.
# A feeding black hole's own parts, every motion worked out on the card: disc-rs, its accretion disc's reach in horizon radii
#   (from the innermost stable orbit, 3, out), each point of gas on its own Kepler orbit (the inner ones round fastest, so the
#   disc shears into spirals) and brightened on the side coming toward you by its near-light speed; inner-orbit-seconds, how
#   long the inner edge takes to go round as watched (the real time is minutes to hours for a supermassive hole: scaled to be
#   seen); swarm-stars and swarm-rs, stars swept round the hole on tight ellipses of those sizes (as the S stars at the Milky
#   Way's heart); eating-share, how many feeding holes are tearing a star apart right now: a star that came too close,
#   stretched along its orbit into a stream of gas that winds in and feeds the disc (a tidal disruption); eating-stars, how many
#   at once (a supermassive hole's disc can hold several, each on its own stream).
# What each body is made of (its card, and the matter you dive into at its surface) comes from its entry: an asteroid's kind
#   (content/small-bodies.unt @asteroid, picked by belt-share at its place in the belt), a Kuiper body's, a comet's, a rogue
#   planet's (content/planets.unt): each with its mix (content/minerals.unt @mix), makeup, density and albedo.
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · content/black-holes.unt · content/small-bodies.unt · content/stars.unt

@place supermassive-black-hole
# feeding-share: how many are feeding (a glowing accretion disc; counting the dim, slow-feeding ones, as most galaxies' are);
#   jets-share: of those feeding, how many fire jets from their poles (radio galaxies, blazars, quasars)
feeding-share: 0.3
jets-share: 0.25
name: A supermassive black hole
where: galaxy-heart
entry: blackhole supermassive
size-km: 3e9
disc-rs: 3..60
inner-orbit-seconds: 8
swarm-stars: 40
swarm-rs: 12..120
eating-share: 0.35
eating-stars: 1..2
colour: 1 0.7 0.35
says: millions to billions of solar masses at the galaxy's heart, its disc of hot gas glowing as it falls in

@place stellar-black-hole
# feeding only with a close companion star to pull gas from (X-ray binaries), a few in a hundred; a third of those fire jets
#   (microquasars, as SS 433)
feeding-share: 0.05
jets-share: 0.35
name: A stellar-mass black hole
where: galaxy
per-cubic-ly: 4e-6
entry: blackhole stellar
size-km: 2e5
disc-rs: 3..40
inner-orbit-seconds: 5
swarm-stars: 0
eating-share: 0
colour: 0.9 0.55 0.3
says: what is left of a star of more than about twenty solar masses; it may still feed on a companion

@place neutron-star
name: A neutron star
where: galaxy
per-cubic-ly: 4e-6
entry: star neutron-star
size-km: 20
colour: 0.75 0.85 1
says: a star's collapsed core, a city across, about one and a half solar masses

@place rogue-planet
name: A rogue planet
where: galaxy
per-cubic-ly: 0.008
entry: planet rogue
size-km: 12000
colour: 0.45 0.45 0.55
says: a planet with no star, thrown out of the system it formed in, wandering the galaxy in the dark

@place intergalactic-star
name: A star between the galaxies
where: between-galaxies
per-cubic-ly: 4e-7
entry: star m-type
size-km: 7e5
colour: 1 0.7 0.5
says: torn from its galaxy long ago (intracluster light): it drifts alone between the galaxies

@place intergalactic-rogue
name: A rogue planet between the galaxies
where: between-galaxies
per-cubic-ly: 8e-7
entry: planet rogue
size-km: 12000
colour: 0.4 0.4 0.5
says: thrown out of its system and out of its galaxy, alone in the dark between galaxies

@place asteroid
name: An asteroid
where: belt
shown: 400
entry: asteroid main-belt
size-km: 30
colour: 0.6 0.55 0.5
says: rock and metal left over from the making of the planets
shape: rock

@place kuiper-object
name: An icy body of the Kuiper belt
where: kuiper
shown: 300
entry: far classical-kbo
size-km: 100
colour: 0.75 0.7 0.7
says: ice and rock beyond the planets, where the comets of short period come from
shape: rock

@place oort-comet
name: A comet of the Oort cloud
where: oort
shown: 300
entry: comet long-period
size-km: 10
colour: 0.8 0.88 1
says: an icy nucleus far out in the star's huge spherical cloud; a nudge sends it falling in as a long-period comet
shape: rock

@place inner-comet
name: A comet near its star
where: inner
shown: 3
entry: comet short-period
size-km: 5
colour: 0.85 0.92 1
shape: comet
says: a nucleus of ice and dust warmed by its star: gas and dust boil off into its coma and are pushed out into its tail
coma-km: 1e5..1e6
tail-km: 1e7..8e7